Gabriel benchmarks in Genera
===========================

suite.lisp is the unmodified, public-domain Symbolics Common Lisp version of
Richard Gabriel's benchmark suite. The installed source was:
  /var/lib/symbolics/rel-8-6/sys.sct/examples/gabriel-benchmarks.lisp.~19~
Its header credits Charlie Hornig and Dan Weinreb. It selects 29 non-I/O
workloads; the 12 FRPOLY cases are separate workloads in that selection.

driver.lisp calls those original timing functions. Each workload gets one
untimed warm-up, then seven timed batches. counts.json fixes the number of
calls in each batch, identically for both interpreters. The counts were
calibrated on this host to target at least 0.4 seconds in the C interpreter;
the quicker Alpha samples also stay well above the timer's resolution.

Timing uses the suite's TIMED-DURATION unchanged: it permits scheduling and
reclaims GC oldspace before timing, then inhibits guest preemption during
the batch. Ephemeral GC is enabled and dynamic GC disabled in both engines.
Warm-up return values are saved separately to compare the engines. File I/O,
compilation, loading, and warm-up are excluded from the reported intervals.

REAL ticks are elapsed time. RUN ticks are Genera's adjusted runtime, not
Linux process CPU time. The comparison uses REAL. Genera's declared rate is
977 ticks/second, approximately 1.024 ms/tick. The driver uses binary writes
for plain ASCII CSV, avoiding Genera's character-style escapes on append.

run.py starts a private Xvfb server with GLX disabled, boots the supplied
binary, logs in as lisp-machine, loads the driver through the existing NFS
export, collects results, and stops only that test instance. It refuses to
start if another genera process is running. It pins Genera and its helper
threads to one logical CPU (default 6), uses nice level 10, disables core
dumps, limits output file size, and imposes a timeout. The desktop display
is not used. Requirements: libX11, Xvfb, ImageMagick's import, taskset, pgrep,
and the already configured TAP interface and guest-accessible NFS export.
Defaults reflect the benchmark machine; paths, CPU, display, and network
can be overridden with command-line options.

Build matching Release versions, changing only VLM_C_ENGINE:

  cmake -S . -B build-bench/c -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DVLM_C_ENGINE=ON
  cmake -S . -B build-bench/alpha -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DVLM_C_ENGINE=OFF
  cmake --build build-bench/c --parallel 2
  cmake --build build-bench/alpha --parallel 2
  ctest --test-dir build-bench/c --output-on-failure
  ctest --test-dir build-bench/alpha --output-on-failure

The default VLM_FAST=ON and VLM_NATIVE=ON apply to both. The direct C core's
existing -fno-fast-math override remains in effect for IEEE semantics.
"Alpha" means the repository's generated Alpha-derived interpreter compiled
for x86-64, not native execution on Alpha hardware.

For a new experiment, choose a fresh directory beneath the existing export
and pass matching --export-dir and --guest-dir values. Compile ONCE with
--compile, on either interpreter, then omit --compile for all measured boots
so they load exactly the same suite.vbin and driver.vbin. Do not overwrite
old compiled output: Genera's NFS file-version handling can fail on repeated
replacement. The runner checks this before launching.

Example preparation run (excluded from reported results):

  python3 benchmarks/gabriel/run.py ./build-bench/c/src/genera \
    --label preparation --compile --samples 1 \
    --counts benchmarks/gabriel/counts.json \
    --export-dir /var/lib/symbolics/new-gabriel-run \
    --guest-dir CHESTNUT:/var/lib/symbolics/new-gabriel-run

Run six fresh boots, in order C1, Alpha1, Alpha2, C2, C3, Alpha3. For each,
use the appropriate binary and --label c-1, alpha-1, alpha-2, c-2, c-3, or
alpha-3. Use the same export/guest paths and these arguments each time:

  --samples 7 --counts benchmarks/gabriel/counts.json --timeout 300

Each label must be new. Data defaults to build-bench/results. Analyze with:

  python3 benchmarks/gabriel/analyze.py build-bench/results

analyze.py validates the complete sample set, batch counts, clock rate, and
matching warm-up return values. It computes each boot's median seconds per
canonical suite call, then the median of the three boot medians. summary.csv
also includes the minimum and maximum boot medians. C/Alpha above 1 means
the C engine takes longer. The aggregate is the geometric mean of the 29
individual time ratios, giving each suite entry equal weight. It is not a
prediction of interactive application performance or startup time.

The dated results directory records the actual hardware, build settings,
binary/world/source hashes, raw timing samples, and conclusions for this run.

Comparing C optimizations
------------------------

VLM_C_ICACHE_ENTRIES sets the C engine's decoded instruction cache size at
configure time. Use the same build flags, guest binaries, counts, and host
settings when comparing sizes. Run variants sequentially, without compiling
on the host during measured runs. Preserve the original executable to make
a subsequent baseline check possible.

compare.py applies the same validation and aggregation to arbitrary groups
of fresh boots. Quote each glob so the shell does not expand it. For example:

  python3 benchmarks/gabriel/compare.py \
    --group 'baseline=build-bench/results/baseline-*.csv' \
    --group 'poll=build-bench/results/poll-*.csv' \
    --group 'cache16k=build-bench/results/cache16k-*.csv' \
    --output build-bench/results/comparison.csv

The first group is the baseline. Speedup is baseline time / variant time;
1.10x means 10% more throughput, or 9.09% less elapsed time. Each group can
have a different number of boots; use repeated boots for final conclusions
and label single-boot cache sizing experiments as exploratory.

results/2026-10-24-optimization records the polling and cache-size experiment,
including separate contributions, repeated measurements, and the comparison
with the original C and Alpha results in results/2026-10-24.
